Select the field_bigint2 backend when HAZYNC_FIELD_BIGINT2=1.

⛔ EXPERIMENTAL, OPT-IN, AND IT MOVES METHOD_ID. Two one-line switch entries; nothing else changes.

libsecp already parameterises its field backend -- field_5x52, field_10x26, asm variants -- so this
uses its own extension point rather than working around it. Every algorithm above the field stays
libsecp's: same wNAF, same GLV, same ECDSA logic, same checks. Only the representation and the five
primitives beneath it change.

⚠ Selected FIRST, ahead of the wide-multiplication choice. SECP256K1_WIDEMUL_INT64 vs INT128 decides
how a 32x32 or 64x64 product is formed in software, which is irrelevant when the multiply is not
performed here at all.

Measured motivation (host field-bench, mode 15, libsecp's own functions, same run):
    software fe_mul                1,167 cycles
    coprocessor, native form          83
    coprocessor, +10x26 conversion   854
The conversion costs 771 cycles, 9.3x the operation, which is why the representation has to be
canonical rather than converted at the boundary. See docs/FIELD_BIGINT2_BACKEND.md.

--- a/src/field.h
+++ b/src/field.h
@@ -37,7 +37,11 @@
 #  define SECP256K1_FE_VERIFY_FIELDS
 #endif
 
-#if defined(SECP256K1_WIDEMUL_INT128)
+#if defined(HAZYNC_FIELD_BIGINT2)
+/* hazync: canonical 8x32 limbs backed by the RISC0 bigint2 coprocessor. Selected FIRST so it wins
+ * over the wide-multiplication choice, which is irrelevant when the multiply is not done here. */
+#include "field_bigint2.h"
+#elif defined(SECP256K1_WIDEMUL_INT128)
 #include "field_5x52.h"
 #elif defined(SECP256K1_WIDEMUL_INT64)
 #include "field_10x26.h"
--- a/src/field_impl.h
+++ b/src/field_impl.h
@@ -10,7 +10,9 @@
 #include "field.h"
 #include "util.h"
 
-#if defined(SECP256K1_WIDEMUL_INT128)
+#if defined(HAZYNC_FIELD_BIGINT2)
+#include "field_bigint2_impl.h"
+#elif defined(SECP256K1_WIDEMUL_INT128)
 #include "field_5x52_impl.h"
 #elif defined(SECP256K1_WIDEMUL_INT64)
 #include "field_10x26_impl.h"
